//!
//! # Per-CPU Data
//!
//! A small block of data that belongs to the processor rather than to a
//! thread, reached through the GS segment base.
//!
//! ## Overview
//!
//! The `SYSCALL` entry starts with nothing it can trust: RSP is still the
//! user's stack pointer and every general register holds a user value. It
//! needs one place to park RSP and one place to load the kernel stack from,
//! and both have to be per processor, because two processors can enter the
//! kernel at the same time. x86_64 offers the GS base for that: an address
//! the processor adds to every `gs:` memory operand, set by an MSR.
//!
//! Ring 3 may have its own GS base, so there are two MSRs and an instruction
//! that exchanges them:
//!
//! ```text
//!                       in ring 0              in ring 3
//!   IA32_GS_BASE        per-CPU block          user GS base (0 here)
//!   IA32_KERNEL_GS_BASE user GS base (0)       per-CPU block
//!
//!   swapgs: exchange the two. Executed once on every way into the kernel
//!   from ring 3 and once on every way out to ring 3:
//!
//!     into ring 0    SYSCALL entry               first instruction
//!                    interrupt or exception      when the saved CS has RPL 3
//!     out to ring 3  SYSRET                      right before it
//!                    IRETQ from the common stub  when the saved CS has RPL 3
//!                    first entry (ignis_enter_user) right before its IRETQ
//! ```
//!
//! An interrupt from ring 0 does not swap: GS already names the block. The
//! saved CS decides on the way out too, so a thread switch inside the
//! handler cannot unbalance it.
//!
//! The block layout is defined in `cpu_local_asm.S`. With one processor
//! there is one block. A second processor would get its own block and its
//! own `initialize` call.
//!
//! An NMI or machine check taken between a `swapgs` and the instruction
//! that changes privilege would run with the wrong GS base. Both are fatal
//! in IgniOS (`Trap::Exception`), so the window costs nothing yet. A kernel
//! that handles them has to find the right base another way, as Linux's
//! "paranoid entry" does.
//!
//! See concept doc 18 and Intel SDM Vol. 2B, "SWAPGS".

import _ from "./cpu";

/// The routines of `cpu_local_asm.S`.
extern CpuLocalAsm {
  function ignis_cpu_local_address(): u64;

  function ignis_cpu_local_self(): u64;
}

/// The per-CPU data API.
namespace Arch::CpuLocal {
  /// MSR number of IA32_GS_BASE, the GS base in effect.
  const GS_BASE_MSR: u32 = 0xC0000101;

  /// MSR number of IA32_KERNEL_GS_BASE, the value `swapgs` exchanges with
  /// IA32_GS_BASE.
  const KERNEL_GS_BASE_MSR: u32 = 0xC0000102;

  /// Fills in the self pointer and makes GS name the block: IA32_GS_BASE
  /// is the block and IA32_KERNEL_GS_BASE, the user's GS base while the
  /// kernel runs, is 0. Runs once in ring 0, before the first entry to
  /// ring 3 and after the GDT load, which resets the GS base.
  function initialize(): void {
    let block: u64 = blockAddress();

    *(block as *mut u64) = block;

    Arch::Cpu::writeMsr(GS_BASE_MSR, block);
    Arch::Cpu::writeMsr(KERNEL_GS_BASE_MSR, 0);
  }

  /// The address of this processor's block.
  function blockAddress(): u64 {
    return CpuLocalAsm::ignis_cpu_local_address();
  }

  /// IA32_GS_BASE. In ring 0 it must be `blockAddress()`.
  function gsBase(): u64 {
    return Arch::Cpu::readMsr(GS_BASE_MSR);
  }

  /// IA32_KERNEL_GS_BASE. In ring 0 it holds the user's GS base.
  function userGsBase(): u64 {
    return Arch::Cpu::readMsr(KERNEL_GS_BASE_MSR);
  }

  /// The self pointer read through `gs:[0]`.
  function selfThroughGs(): u64 {
    return CpuLocalAsm::ignis_cpu_local_self();
  }
}
